igidity.", "tags": [ "engineering" ], "status": "active" }, { "id": "ENG-000002", "category": "Engineering", "subcategory": "Mechanics", "difficulty": "easy", "question": "What is the measure of the rotational force applied around an axis?", "options": [ "Torque", "Velocity", "Momentum", "Pressure" ], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Torque is rotational mechanical force (Force × Distance).", "tags": [ "engineering" ], "status": "active" }, { "id": "ENG-000003", "category": "Engineering", "subcategory": "Materials", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "According to Hooke's Law within elastic limits, stress is directly proportional to what?", "options": [ "Strain", "Temperature", "Volume", "Density" ], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Stress is proportional to strain (σ = E · ε).", "tags": [ "engineering" ], "status": "active" }, { "id": "ENG-000004", "category": "Engineering", "subcategory": "Thermodynamics", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "Which law of thermodynamics states that entropy in an isolated system always increases?", "options": [ "Second Law of Thermodynamics", "First Law", "Third Law", "Zeroth Law" ], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The Second Law dictates spontaneous entropy increase.", "tags": [ "engineering" ], "status": "active" }, { "id": "ENG-000005", "category": "Engineering", "subcategory": "Fluids", "difficulty": "hard", "question": "Which dimensionless number predicts laminar vs turbulent fluid flow?", "options": [ "Reynolds Number (Re)", "Mach Number", "Froude Number", "Prandtl Number" ], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Reynolds number characterizes fluid turbulence.", "tags": [ "engineering" ], "status": "active" }, { "id": "ENG-000006", "category": "Engineering", "subcategory": "Statics", "difficulty": "hard", "question": "In beam mechanics, what does the first derivative of the bending moment equation represent?", "options": [ "Shear Force (V)", "Deflection (y)", "Slope (θ)", "Distributed Load (w)" ], "answer": 0, "explanation": "dM/dx = V (Shear Force).", "tags": [ "engineering" ], "status": "active" }, { "id": "ENG-000007", "category": "Engineering", "subcategory": "Dynamics", "difficulty": "easy", "question": "What is Newton's Second Law of Motion?", "options": [ "Force = Mass × Acceleration (F = ma)", "For every action there is equal and opposite reaction", "Inertia maintains constant velocity", "Energy cannot be destroyed" ], "answer": 0, "explanation": "F = m · a is Newton's second law.", "tags": [ "engineering" ], "status": "active" }, { "id": "ENG-000008", "category": "Engineering", "subcategory": "Materials", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "What is the point on a stress-strain curve where plastic (permanent) deformation begins?", "options": [ "Yield Point (Yield Strength)", "Ultimate Tensile Strength", "Fracture Point", "Elastic Limit" ], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The yield point marks the boundary between elastic and permanent plastic deformation.", "tags": [ "engineering" ], "status": "active" }, { "id": "ENG-000009", "category": "Engineering", "subcategory": "Thermodynamics", "difficulty": "hard", "question": "What is the theoretical maximum thermodynamic efficiency of any heat engine operating between temperatures T_hot and T_cold?", "options": [ "Carnot Efficiency = 1 - (T_cold / T_hot)", "Rankine Efficiency", "Otto Efficiency = 1 - (1 / r^(γ-1))", "Diesel Efficiency" ], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The Carnot cycle defines the absolute upper efficiency limit: η = 1 - T_C/T_H.", "tags": [ "engineering" ], "status": "active" }, { "id": "ENG-000010", "category": "Engineering", "subcategory": "Energy", "difficulty": "easy", "question": "What is the SI unit of mechanical work and energy?", "options": [ "Joule (J)", "Watt (W)", "Newton (N)", "Pascal (Pa)" ], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Work and energy are measured in Joules (1 N · m).", "tags": [ "engineering" ], "status": "active" }, { "id": "ENG-000011", "category": "Engineering", "subcategory": "Thermodynamics", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "What is the First Law of Thermodynamics fundamentally known as?", "options": [ "The Law of Conservation of Energy", "The Law of Entropy Increase", "The Zeroth Law of Thermal Equilibrium", "The Ideal Gas Law" ], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Energy cannot be created or destroyed, only transformed.", "tags": [ "engineering" ], "status": "active" }, { "id": "ENG-000012", "category": "Engineering", "subcategory": "Materials", "difficulty": "hard", "question": "What test is used to measure a material's impact toughness and resistance to brittle fracture?", "options": [ "Charpy V-Notch Impact Test", "Brinell Hardness Test", "Tensile Yield Test", "Creep Rupture Test" ], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Charpy tests measure energy absorbed during high-strain impact.", "tags": [ "engineering" ], "status": "active" }, { "id": "ENGINEERING-EASY-001-1", "category": "Engineering", "subcategory": "Engineering", "difficulty": "easy", "question": "Which term is best described by the following definition: internal force per unit area within a material?", "options": [ "Forging", "Finite element method", "Torque", "Stress" ], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The correct answer is Stress. It matches the defining characteristic or role described in the question.", "tags": [ "engineering" ], "status": "active" }, { "id": "ENGINEERING-MEDIUM-001-2", "category": "Engineering", "subcategory": "Engineering", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "Which statement best describes Stress?", "options": [ "a numerical technique that divides a complex domain into smaller elements for analysis", "internal force per unit area within a material", "the study of energy, heat, work, and related transformations", "the rotational effect of a force about an axis" ], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The correct answer is internal force per unit area within a material. It matches the defining characteristic or role described in the question.", "tags": [ "engineering" ], "status": "active" }, { "id": "ENGINEERING-